    "hook-snail",
    "plan",
    "when",
    "when-flat",
    "when-catch",
    "when-spikes",
    "when-not"
  ],
  "text": "Take a table that gains eight thousand rows a day. Rebuild all of it every night, and two years in, each run reprocesses over six million rows to add eight thousand. An incremental model processes only the new ones. Seven questions on how it does that and where it goes wrong: which rows count as new, what makes a rerun safe, and why the full rebuild never quite goes away.\n\nQuestion one. When do you use an incremental model, and what's the catch? When a table is large and mostly appends, like events, logs or page views, so a full rebuild is too slow or too expensive. An incremental run processes one day's arrivals, so its cost stays flat while the rebuild's climbs. By day seven hundred thirty, the rebuild does seven hundred fifty-five times the work. The catch is what that shortcut assumes: a reliable way to find the new rows, correct handling of rows that arrive late or change, and a tested way to recover when the table drifts. Recovery means a full rebuild, and its cost grows too. Each spike here is a scheduled full refresh, every sixty days. So when do you choose incremental over a full table rebuild? Only when the table is big and append-mostly. If it's small, or changes broadly every run, a plain table is simpler and safer.",
